WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:00.763
Welcome.

2
00:00:00.763 --> 00:00:04.390
This is the executive briefing
for Zero Trust and Assessment,

3
00:00:04.390 --> 00:00:07.662
one of the books of the
Federal Modernization Workbook.

4
00:00:07.662 --> 00:00:12.761
It runs about thirty minutes, and it's for
the people who make the calls: the CISO,

5
00:00:12.761 --> 00:00:17.625
the authorizing officials, the CIO,
and the Zero Trust implementation lead.

6
00:00:17.625 --> 00:00:20.337
The argument fits in three sentences.

7
00:00:20.337 --> 00:00:25.937
Zero Trust maturity is a property of the
whole agency, not of any product or tenant.

8
00:00:25.937 --> 00:00:30.077
The federal documents set specific
actions and configurations,

9
00:00:30.077 --> 00:00:34.600
but none of them defines a Zero Trust
score or requires a maturity stage.

10
00:00:34.600 --> 00:00:37.441
And the assessment tools
produce evidence for

11
00:00:37.441 --> 00:00:40.137
named people to act on,
not verdicts to report.

12
00:00:40.137 --> 00:00:44.705
I'll take four questions in order: why
Zero Trust belongs to the whole agency,

13
00:00:44.705 --> 00:00:48.756
what the mandates and tools actually
require, who owns the posture,

14
00:00:48.756 --> 00:00:53.175
and how to turn findings into a program
the authorizing official can see.

15
00:00:53.175 --> 00:00:57.725
Every decision slide names the decision,
its owner, and when it's needed.

16
00:00:57.725 --> 00:01:00.460
Let's start with the
usual assessment calendar.
